STEP 3 — Define IntelligenceRequest

Think of this as:

> "A user asked AIFRED a question about this particular mix state."



Something conceptually like:

struct IntelligenceRequest
{
    RequestId requestId;

    std::string question;

    ProfileId profileId;

    ObservationIdentity observationIdentity;

    ReferenceState reference;

    FilteredMixContext context;

    ConversationTurn conversation;
};

The exact types should follow whatever identity types already exist in AIFRED.

Do not invent a second identity system.

That's one of the architectural rules:

> every contract needs enough identity to reject stale/incompatible data.




---

STEP 4 — Define evidence

This is one of the most important pieces of the entire system.

AIFRED needs to know:

> "Why am I allowed to say this?"



So create something conceptually like:

enum class ClaimClass
{
    fact,
    supportedInference,
    possibleExplanation,
    unknown
};

Those four categories are explicitly defined by the architecture.

Then:

struct EvidenceRef
{
    std::string source;
    std::string field;
    std::string observationId;
};

And:

struct Claim
{
    ClaimClass classification;
    std::string text;

    std::vector<EvidenceRef> evidence;

    float confidence;
};

Now AIFRED can distinguish:

Fact

The mix has a low stereo correlation value.

Directly measured.

Supported inference

This suggests the stereo image may have reduced mono compatibility.

Reasonable interpretation.

Possible explanation

A wide chorus, phase rotation, or other stereo processing could contribute.

Possible, but not proven.

Unknown

AIFRED cannot determine which plugin caused this behavior from the available data.

That's exactly the kind of honesty you want.


