STEP 9 — Build Policy

Now:

models/aifred/intelligence/policy/

This is where AIFRED determines:

> "What am I actually allowed to conclude?"



The four claim classes become your backbone.

Your policy engine should answer things like:

canMakeClaim(...)
canCompareReference(...)
isObservationFresh(...)
isEvidenceSufficient(...)
canRecommend(...)

For example:

Observation says:
correlation = -0.4

Allowed:
"Stereo correlation is relatively low."

Potentially allowed:
"This may reduce mono compatibility."

NOT allowed:
"Your chorus plugin is causing phase cancellation."

Because AIFRED doesn't know that.

That distinction is the heart of the intelligence layer.


---

STEP 10 — Implement "no change indicated"

This is actually a huge deal.

You don't want AIFRED to become:

> "AI that always finds something wrong."



The repo explicitly says AIFRED must be able to conclude that the mix is behaving well and no correction is indicated.

So make this a first-class response state:

enum class RecommendationState
{
    investigate,
    recommendation,
    noChangeIndicated,
    insufficientEvidence
};

This is better than forcing every response into:

PROBLEM → FIX


