STEP 13 — Build IntelligenceCore

Now finally:

models/aifred/intelligence/core/

This is the conductor.

The README gives its intended responsibilities very clearly.

Your flow should be:

IntelligenceRequest
        ↓
IntelligenceCore
        ↓
validate request
        ↓
ContextBuilder
        ↓
ToolRegistry
        ↓
Policy
        ↓
Provider
        ↓
ProviderResponse
        ↓
ResponseValidator
        ↓
IntelligenceResponse

In pseudocode:

IntelligenceResponse IntelligenceCore::answer(
    const IntelligenceRequest& request)
{
    validateRequest(request);

    auto context =
        contextBuilder.build(request);

    auto availableTools =
        tools.describeAvailable(context);

    auto policy =
        policyEngine.buildPolicy(context);

    auto providerRequest =
        promptBuilder.build(
            request,
            context,
            availableTools,
            policy
        );

    auto providerResponse =
        provider.reason(providerRequest);

    auto validated =
        responseValidator.validate(
            providerResponse,
            context,
            policy
        );

    return validated;
}

That is the centerpiece.


---

STEP 14 — The response validator is your hallucination firewall

This is probably the most important component after IntelligenceCore.

The repo literally describes response/ as the:

> hallucination firewall between model output and user-visible guidance.



The model says:

Your low end is weak because your kick is masking your bass.

The validator asks:

Do we have evidence that:
    low end is weak?
        YES

Do we have evidence that:
    kick is masking bass?
        NO

Therefore:
    reject/rewrite causal claim.

That's the architecture you want.

The model is not trusted.

The model proposes reasoning.

AIFRED validates it.


